    "body": "Hi @linuxguy123, thanks for the clear report and the screenshots. They made this easy to reproduce.\n\n**What's going on:** in CamCraft the tool always cuts along the machine's Z axis. Turning a setup's WCS onto another face moved the WCS triad on screen, but toolpath generation ignored the turn. So Setup2's facing machined the face that lies flat in the model, not the face your WCS showed, and still reported \"OK\". For the same reason, your top-face pick was refused as \"not horizontal\": it is vertical on the machine. That refusal also sent you toward a 2D Contour, which wasn't the right advice.\n\n**Was any wrong G-code at risk?** No. The post already refuses any rotated work offset by name (#315), so this program could not have been posted. Still, a toolpath shown as OK on the wrong face is exactly the kind of quiet wrong answer we don't allow.\n\n**The fix (PR #1518):** generating in a milling setup whose WCS Z is tilted off the machine's Z is now refused, and the message says why. A facing pick on a face that is level only in the tilted WCS now says the WCS is the cause.\n\n**How to machine Setup2 in the new orientation today:** leave Setup2's WCS Z pointing up and turn the **part** instead, with Setup2's **Part alignment** (lay it on a face or pick axes). The stock, the heights and the facing then follow the new orientation. One limit: in an aligned setup, Face, 2D Contour, 2D Pocket and 2D Adaptive work on the whole model or the stock, but picked faces are not supported there yet.\n\nFor the maintainer: by the brief's split this is a display/workflow defect (a), not wrong output (b). The generated path ignored the WCS tilt, but posting was already blocked by the rotated-offset clause, and the collision and stroke checks ran on the path that was actually drawn. The P1 label stays as set.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
