    "body": "Triage (maintainer, 2026-11-13, in chat): \"not sure that is a bug actually. You should orient the part to Z using Setup.. Not let the WCS orient the part to Z..\"\n\nReading of the report against that:\n\n- **Intended workflow:** the part is turned for a setup with the setup's Part alignment (issue 326). The WCS sets the origin and the squared-up X/Y of the work offset; it does not turn the part to the machine's Z. The reporter's Setup2 used the WCS to turn the part, which is not how a setup is oriented here.\n- **What was a defect:** the WCS pane let the WCS be tilted, drew it tilted, then generation ignored the tilt and reported \"OK\" on a path cut on the face that lies flat in the model. A wrong path with no message is a defect whichever workflow is intended (the post already refused a rotated work offset, so no G-code could post).\n- **What PR 1518 did (merged, `908563656`):** it does not make the WCS orient the part. A milling setup whose WCS Z is tilted off the machine's Z is now refused at generation by name, and the message points to Part alignment. A WCS turned only about Z still generates. That matches the maintainer's view; generating in the WCS frame was considered and dropped (`docs/decisions/1510-tilted-wcs.md`).\n\nSo: user error in the workflow, plus a missing refusal that is now in place. The issue stays closed.\n\nOpen point for the maintainer: the WCS pane still lets a milling WCS be tilted (and then refuses at generate). If the pane should not offer the tilt at all for a milling setup, or should offer \"use Part alignment instead\" at that moment, that is a separate follow-up issue.\n",
    "author_association": "OWNER",
